-- Q1: medium / L2 — thin capitalization as debt-vs-equity indicator
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Whitfield lends $900,000 to his wholly owned C corporation, which has total equity of $50,000. The loan has no fixed maturity date, and repayments are made only when the corporation has sufficient earnings. The IRS challenges the characterization of the advance. What is the most likely outcome?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The advance is recharacterized as an equity contribution, and interest payments are treated as constructive dividends", "The advance is treated as bona fide debt because the shareholder has a legal right to repayment", "The advance is treated as a gift to the corporation with no tax consequences", "The advance is partially debt and partially equity based on a 4:1 safe harbor ratio"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under debt-vs-equity analysis, courts consider factors including the debt-to-equity ratio, fixed maturity, and unconditional repayment obligation. An 18:1 debt-to-equity ratio with no maturity date and earnings-contingent repayments strongly indicates equity disguised as debt. Interest payments would be reclassified as nondeductible constructive dividends. Wrong (B): The absence of a fixed maturity and contingent repayments undermine the claim of bona fide debt regardless of the shareholder's intent. Wrong (C): An advance from a shareholder is not treated as a gift; it is either debt or equity. Wrong (D): There is no statutory 4:1 safe harbor ratio for debt-to-equity classification.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Section 318 family attribution excludes siblings
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 318 constructive ownership rules, which family member's stock is NOT attributed to the taxpayer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Spouse", "Brother", "Child", "Parent"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 318(a)(1) attributes stock ownership among family members including spouse, children, grandchildren, and parents. Siblings are specifically excluded from family attribution under Section 318. This is a frequently tested distinction on the CPA exam. Wrong (A): A spouse's stock is attributed to the taxpayer under Section 318(a)(1). Wrong (C): A child's stock is attributed to the taxpayer under Section 318(a)(1). Wrong (D): A parent's stock is attributed to the taxpayer under Section 318(a)(1).$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Section 304 brother-sister redemption treated as distribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Keane owns 80% of Alpha Corp and 70% of Beta Corp. Keane sells 100 shares of Alpha Corp stock (basis $25,000) to Beta Corp for $90,000 cash. Under Section 304(a)(1), how is this transaction treated for tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As a sale producing $65,000 long-term capital gain", "As a Section 301 distribution of $90,000 tested against the combined E&P of both corporations, with any excess reducing Keane's Alpha stock basis", "As a tax-free Section 351 exchange because Keane controls Beta Corp", "As a partial liquidation of Alpha Corp under Section 302(b)(4)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 304(a)(1), when a shareholder controlling two corporations sells stock of one to the other, the transaction is treated as a redemption by the acquiring corporation (Beta). The redemption is tested under Section 302(b); if none of the tests are met, it is treated as a Section 301 distribution. The distribution is measured against the combined E&P of both corporations (acquiring corporation first). Any amount exceeding E&P reduces stock basis, and further excess is capital gain. Wrong (A): Section 304 prevents sale treatment when the seller controls both corporations. Wrong (C): Section 351 applies to property-for-stock exchanges, not stock-for-cash sales between controlled corporations. Wrong (D): Partial liquidation applies at the corporate level for genuine business contractions, not cross-entity stock sales.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Section 302 failed test with basis transfer to remaining shares
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Drake owns 200 of 400 outstanding shares of Falcon Corp (50%), with a basis of $80,000. His adult son Owen owns the remaining 200 shares. Falcon redeems 50 of Drake's shares for $100,000. After applying Section 318 constructive ownership, Drake is deemed to own 100% before and 100% after the redemption (150 directly + 200 attributed from Owen = 350 of 350). None of the Section 302(b) tests are met. Drake should:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Report $100,000 as long-term capital gain on the redeemed shares", "Report $80,000 as long-term capital gain and $20,000 as return of basis", "Report $100,000 as a tax-free return of basis reducing stock basis to zero", "Report $100,000 as a dividend to the extent of E&P, adding the redeemed shares' $20,000 basis to remaining shares"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): When no Section 302(b) test is met, the redemption is treated as an IRC Section 301 distribution — taxable as a dividend to the extent of corporate E&P. Per Treas. Reg. Section 1.302-2(c), the basis of the redeemed shares ($80,000 x 50/200 = $20,000) is added to the basis of the shareholder's remaining shares. Wrong (A): Capital gain treatment requires satisfying a Section 302(b) test, which was not met here. Wrong (B): This incorrectly applies sale/exchange treatment to a failed Section 302 redemption. Wrong (C): The distribution is not tax-free; it is a dividend to the extent of E&P under Section 301.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — constructive dividend from personal use of corporate auto
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Grant, the sole shareholder of a C corporation, uses a corporate-owned vehicle exclusively for personal errands. The annual FMV of the personal use is $12,000. The corporation does not include this amount on Grant's W-2 or report it as a distribution. What is the tax treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $12,000 is a constructive dividend to Grant, taxable as dividend income to the extent of corporate E&P", "The $12,000 is deductible by the corporation as a business expense", "Grant has no income because the corporation owns the vehicle", "The personal use is treated as a tax-free fringe benefit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Personal use of corporate property by a shareholder is a constructive dividend. The IRS treats the FMV of the personal benefit ($12,000) as a distribution taxable as dividend income to the extent of corporate E&P. The corporation loses any deduction for the personal-use portion. Wrong (B): Personal use of corporate property is not deductible as a business expense. Wrong (C): Corporate ownership does not shield the shareholder from income recognition on personal use. Wrong (D): Personal use of a corporate vehicle by a shareholder-owner is not an excludable fringe benefit.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Section 302(b)(3) complete termination with family attribution waiver
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Helena owns 300 of 1,000 shares of Orion Corp (30%), and her daughter Vera owns 200 shares (20%). Orion redeems all 300 of Helena's shares for $450,000 (basis $90,000). Under Section 318, Helena constructively owns Vera's 200 shares, giving her 20% ownership after the redemption. Helena wants capital gain treatment. She files an agreement under Section 302(c)(2) to waive family attribution, retains no interest in Orion (other than as a creditor for installment payments), and agrees to notify the IRS of any reacquisition within 10 years. The redemption qualifies as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A dividend to the extent of E&P because Helena still constructively owns 20%", "A complete termination under Section 302(b)(3), resulting in a $360,000 capital gain", "A substantially disproportionate redemption under Section 302(b)(2)", "A partial liquidation under Section 302(b)(4)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 302(c)(2) allows a shareholder to waive family attribution for purposes of the complete termination test under Section 302(b)(3), provided the shareholder retains no interest in the corporation (except as a creditor), files a written agreement, and agrees to a 10-year notification period. Helena meets all conditions, so the redemption qualifies as a complete termination with $360,000 capital gain ($450,000 − $90,000). Wrong (A): Family attribution can be waived under Section 302(c)(2) if the shareholder meets the statutory requirements. Wrong (C): The substantially disproportionate test is irrelevant when the complete termination test is satisfied. Wrong (D): Partial liquidation applies at the corporate level and requires a corporate contraction, which is not present here.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Section 751(b) hot asset ordinary income in partnership distribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Waverly Partnership holds two assets: accounts receivable (basis $0, FMV $80,000) and land (basis $60,000, FMV $120,000). Waverly makes a current distribution of the land to equal partner Shelton in exchange for a reduction in Shelton's partnership interest. Under Section 751(b), how is Shelton's receipt of the land treated to the extent it represents a disproportionate distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Entirely as a tax-free current distribution under Section 731", "Shelton is treated as receiving a proportionate share of hot assets in a taxable exchange, with the excess land as a distribution", "As capital gain on the full FMV of the land because land is a capital asset", "As a Section 1031 like-kind exchange of partnership interest for real property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 751(b), when a distribution changes a partner's share of hot assets (unrealized receivables or substantially appreciated inventory), the transaction is bifurcated. Shelton is treated as first receiving a proportionate share of the hot assets (receivables) in a taxable exchange, generating ordinary income, and then receiving the remaining land as a distribution governed by Section 731-733. Wrong (A): Section 751(b) overrides general distribution rules when hot assets are disproportionately shifted. Wrong (C): The ordinary income component from hot assets prevents pure capital gain treatment on the entire distribution. Wrong (D): Section 1031 does not apply to partnership interest exchanges after the 2017 TCJA, and the hot asset rules take priority regardless.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L2 — Section 1041 transfer incident to divorce is tax-free
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Pursuant to a divorce decree, Morgan transfers stock with a basis of $30,000 and FMV of $95,000 to her ex-spouse Quinn within one year of the divorce. Under Section 1041, what are the tax consequences of this transfer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Morgan recognizes $65,000 capital gain and Quinn takes a $95,000 FMV basis", "Morgan recognizes no gain or loss, and Quinn takes Morgan's $30,000 carryover basis", "Both Morgan and Quinn split the $65,000 gain equally at $32,500 each", "The transfer is taxable because it occurs after the marriage has ended"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1041, transfers of property between spouses or incident to divorce are treated as gifts — no gain or loss is recognized by the transferor. The recipient takes a carryover basis equal to the transferor's adjusted basis ($30,000). A transfer is incident to divorce if it occurs within one year of the divorce or is related to the cessation of the marriage. Wrong (A): Section 1041 prevents gain recognition regardless of the appreciation in the property. Wrong (C): There is no gain-splitting mechanism; the entire transfer is non-recognition. Wrong (D): Section 1041 specifically covers transfers incident to divorce, not just transfers during marriage.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — constructive dividend from bargain sale to shareholder
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Cedar Corp sells a parcel of land with an FMV of $250,000 to its sole shareholder, Prescott, for $100,000. Cedar Corp has accumulated E&P of $500,000. What is the tax consequence to Prescott?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A $150,000 constructive dividend taxable as dividend income, with Prescott taking a $250,000 basis in the land", "No income to Prescott because the land was purchased in an arm's-length transaction", "Prescott has $150,000 of ordinary income and takes a $100,000 cost basis in the land", "Prescott has a $250,000 long-term capital gain on the discounted land purchase"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): A bargain sale from a corporation to its shareholder results in a constructive dividend equal to the difference between FMV and the price paid ($250,000 − $100,000 = $150,000). The dividend is taxable to the extent of E&P. Prescott's basis in the land is $250,000 ($100,000 paid + $150,000 dividend amount). Wrong (B): A sale below FMV to a controlling shareholder is not arm's-length and triggers constructive dividend treatment. Wrong (C): The constructive dividend is taxed as dividend income, not ordinary income, and the basis includes the dividend portion. Wrong (D): This is not a capital gain transaction; it is a constructive dividend from the corporation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — Section 318 option attribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Rivera owns no stock in Apex Corp but holds an option to purchase 100 of Apex's 400 outstanding shares. Under Section 318 constructive ownership rules, Rivera is treated as owning what percentage of Apex Corp?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["0% because options are not treated as actual stock ownership", "25% because the option covers 100 of 400 outstanding shares", "100% because holding any option attributes all outstanding shares", "50% because options are attributed at double the proportional rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 318(a)(4), a person holding an option to acquire stock is treated as owning that stock for constructive ownership purposes. Rivera's option to purchase 100 of 400 shares means she is deemed to own 25% (100/400). Wrong (A): Section 318(a)(4) specifically treats options as constructive ownership of the underlying stock. Wrong (C): Rivera only has an option for 100 of 400 shares, not all outstanding shares. Wrong (D): The option covers 100 of 400 shares (25%), not 200 of 400 shares (50%).$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — corporation paying shareholder personal expenses as constructive dividend
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Dalton Corp, a C corporation with $300,000 of accumulated E&P, pays $45,000 for home renovations on the personal residence of its sole shareholder, Harper. The payment is recorded as a miscellaneous business expense on the corporate books. What is the correct tax treatment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The corporation deducts $45,000 and Harper has no income", "The corporation deducts $45,000 as compensation expense to Harper", "The $45,000 is a constructive dividend to Harper, not deductible by Dalton Corp", "The $45,000 is a tax-free loan from Dalton Corp to Harper"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): When a corporation pays personal expenses of a shareholder, the payment is treated as a constructive dividend to the extent of E&P. Dalton Corp has sufficient E&P ($300,000), so the full $45,000 is dividend income to Harper. The corporation cannot deduct constructive dividends. Wrong (A): Personal expenses of a shareholder are not deductible business expenses. Wrong (B): Unless properly documented as compensation for services, personal expense payments are constructive dividends, not compensation. Wrong (D): There is no loan structure here; the corporation paid expenses without any repayment obligation.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Section 302(b)(1) meaningful reduction test
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Fleming owns 27% of Zenith Corp. The corporation redeems enough shares to reduce Fleming's ownership to 22%. The redemption does not qualify under the substantially disproportionate test of Section 302(b)(2). Under Section 302(b)(1), this redemption is most likely treated as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A dividend because any reduction of less than 10 percentage points is automatically a dividend", "A tax-free return of capital to Fleming regardless of Zenith's E&P balance", "A complete termination of Fleming's entire stock interest in Zenith Corp", "A sale or exchange because the 27%-to-22% reduction is a meaningful reduction in interest"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 302(b)(1) provides sale/exchange treatment if the redemption is "not essentially equivalent to a dividend," determined by whether there is a meaningful reduction in the shareholder's proportionate interest (U.S. v. Davis). A reduction from 27% to 22% in a minority shareholder's interest has been held to constitute a meaningful reduction, qualifying for capital gain treatment. Wrong (A): There is no automatic percentage-point threshold; Section 302(b)(1) is a facts-and-circumstances test. Wrong (B): Return of capital treatment is not the framework; the question is sale/exchange vs. dividend. Wrong (C): Complete termination under Section 302(b)(3) requires redemption of all shares, not a partial reduction.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Section 1239 recharacterizes gain as ordinary on depreciable property sale to related party
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Nguyen sells a building (Section 1250 property) with a basis of $120,000 and FMV of $310,000 to a corporation she controls (80% ownership). Under Section 1239, how is the $190,000 gain characterized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$190,000 long-term capital gain because real property sales produce capital gain", "$190,000 Section 1231 gain subject to depreciation recapture only to the extent of prior depreciation", "$190,000 ordinary income because the property is depreciable in the hands of the related buyer", "$190,000 is deferred until the corporation sells the building to an unrelated party"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1239, gain on the sale of depreciable property between related parties (including a taxpayer and a controlled corporation with more than 50% ownership) is treated as ordinary income rather than capital gain or Section 1231 gain. The entire $190,000 gain is ordinary income because the building is depreciable property in the hands of the buyer corporation. Wrong (A): Section 1239 overrides capital gain treatment for sales of depreciable property to related parties. Wrong (B): Section 1239 recharacterizes the entire gain as ordinary, not just the depreciation recapture portion. Wrong (D): There is no deferral mechanism; the gain is recognized currently but as ordinary income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — S corp distribution exceeding AAA + E&P + basis triggers gain
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Hartley is the sole shareholder of an S corporation formerly a C corporation. The AAA is $30,000, accumulated C corporation E&P is $20,000, and Hartley's stock basis is $40,000. The corporation distributes $110,000 to Hartley. Evaluate how the distribution layers affect Hartley's total tax liability.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$110,000 taxable dividend because the distribution exceeds both AAA and stock basis combined", "$30,000 tax-free (AAA), $20,000 taxable dividend (E&P), $10,000 tax-free (return of basis), $50,000 capital gain", "$30,000 tax-free (AAA), $20,000 taxable dividend (E&P), $40,000 tax-free return of basis, $20,000 capital gain", "$50,000 tax-free (AAA + E&P combined), $10,000 return of basis, and $50,000 capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1368(c), the $110,000 distribution layers: $30,000 from AAA (tax-free, reduces basis from $40,000 to $10,000), $20,000 from accumulated E&P (taxable dividend, basis unchanged), $10,000 return of remaining basis (tax-free, reduces basis to $0), and $50,000 excess taxed as capital gain under Section 1368(b)(2). Wrong (A): The distribution is not entirely a dividend; it follows the four-layer ordering under Section 1368(c). Wrong (C): This incorrectly treats the full $40,000 original basis as available after the AAA distribution already reduced it to $10,000. Wrong (D): AAA and E&P are separate layers with different tax treatment; they cannot be combined.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — constructive dividend definition
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Which of the following best describes a constructive dividend?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A formally declared dividend approved by the board of directors", "A cash distribution made during a corporate liquidation", "An economic benefit conferred on a shareholder that is not formally declared as a dividend but is recharacterized as one by the IRS", "A stock dividend issued to all shareholders proportionally"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): A constructive dividend occurs when a corporation confers an economic benefit on a shareholder without formally declaring a dividend. The IRS recharacterizes the transaction as a dividend to the extent of the corporation's E&P. Common examples include excessive compensation, personal use of corporate property, and bargain sales. Wrong (A): A formally declared dividend is an actual dividend, not a constructive one. Wrong (B): Liquidating distributions are governed by Sections 331 and 336, not constructive dividend rules. Wrong (D): Stock dividends are actual distributions of stock, not constructive dividends.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — Section 302(b)(4) partial liquidation definition
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 302(b)(4), a partial liquidation resulting in sale or exchange treatment requires which corporate-level event?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The shareholder must own less than 50% of total voting power after the redemption", "The corporation must have no accumulated E&P", "Family attribution under Section 318 must be waived", "The distribution must be attributable to a genuine contraction of the corporation's business"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 302(b)(4) grants sale/exchange treatment for redemptions that qualify as partial liquidations. The test is applied at the corporate level and requires the distribution to be not essentially equivalent to a dividend, typically involving a genuine contraction of the corporation's business, such as terminating one of two active businesses. Wrong (A): The 50% voting power test relates to Section 302(b)(2) (substantially disproportionate), not partial liquidation. Wrong (B): Accumulated E&P is not a requirement for partial liquidation treatment. Wrong (C): Family attribution waiver relates to Section 302(b)(3) (complete termination), not partial liquidation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — IRC 704(c) built-in gain allocation to contributing partner
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Carlisle contributes equipment with a basis of $40,000 and FMV of $100,000 to an equal three-partner partnership. Two years later, the partnership sells the equipment for $110,000. Under IRC Section 704(c), how is the $70,000 total gain ($110,000 − $40,000) allocated among the partners?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Carlisle is allocated $63,333 of gain ($60,000 built-in gain plus one-third of the $10,000 post-contribution gain), and each other partner is allocated $3,333", "Each partner is allocated $23,333 of gain (one-third each)", "Carlisle is allocated $70,000 of total gain because he contributed the property", "Carlisle is allocated $40,000 and the remaining $30,000 is split equally"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 704(c), the $60,000 built-in gain ($100,000 FMV − $40,000 basis at contribution) must be allocated entirely to Carlisle, the contributing partner. The remaining $10,000 of post-contribution appreciation ($110,000 − $100,000) is shared equally: $3,333 each. Total: Carlisle gets $63,333, each other partner gets $3,333. Wrong (B): Equal allocation ignores the Section 704(c) requirement to allocate pre-contribution gain to the contributing partner. Wrong (C): Only the built-in gain is allocated entirely to Carlisle; post-contribution gain is shared. Wrong (D): This allocation does not follow any recognized method under Section 704(c).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — shareholder loans reclassified when no intent to repay
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Bennett withdraws $180,000 from his wholly owned C corporation. The transaction is recorded as a shareholder loan, but there is no promissory note, no repayment schedule, no interest charged, and Bennett has never repaid any prior advances. The corporation has $200,000 of accumulated E&P. The IRS will most likely reclassify this transaction as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A tax-free return of capital to Bennett", "A constructive dividend of $180,000 taxable to Bennett", "A bona fide loan with imputed interest under Section 7872", "Compensation income to Bennett"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): When a shareholder withdraws funds with no promissory note, no repayment schedule, no interest, and a history of non-repayment, the IRS treats the advance as a constructive dividend rather than a loan. Since the corporation has sufficient E&P ($200,000), the entire $180,000 is taxable as dividend income. Wrong (A): Return of capital treatment requires a formal distribution that exceeds E&P, which is not the case here. Wrong (C): Section 7872 applies to bona fide loans; without intent to repay, the transaction is not a loan at all. Wrong (D): Compensation treatment would require a services relationship and would be deductible by the corporation, whereas the IRS typically treats unexplained shareholder withdrawals as dividends.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Section 318 entity-to-owner attribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 318(a)(2)(C), how is stock owned by a corporation attributed to its shareholders?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock is attributed equally to all shareholders regardless of ownership percentage", "Stock is attributed to all shareholders who own at least 10% of the corporation", "Stock is attributed proportionally to any shareholder who owns 50% or more of the corporation's stock", "Stock is attributed to the corporation's board of directors only"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 318(a)(2)(C), stock owned by a corporation is attributed proportionally to any shareholder who owns 50% or more (by value) of the corporation's stock. Shareholders owning less than 50% do not receive entity-to-owner attribution from a corporation. Wrong (A): Attribution is not equal; it is proportional and applies only to 50%+ shareholders. Wrong (B): The threshold is 50% ownership, not 10%. Wrong (D): Attribution flows to qualifying shareholders, not to the board of directors.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Section 267(d) disallowed loss cannot create or increase a loss on resale
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Dawson sells land with a basis of $90,000 to his wholly owned corporation for $60,000. The $30,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267. The corporation later sells the land to an unrelated buyer for $55,000. Under Section 267(d), how much gain or loss does the corporation recognize?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$5,000 loss recognized on the resale to the unrelated buyer", "$25,000 loss after applying the previously disallowed loss against basis", "$35,000 loss computed using Dawson's original $90,000 basis in the land", "$0 gain or loss because the disallowed loss cannot create or increase a loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 267(d), the related buyer may use the previously disallowed loss only to reduce gain on a subsequent sale, but it cannot create or increase a loss. The corporation's realized loss is $5,000 ($55,000 − $60,000 basis). The disallowed $30,000 loss cannot increase this loss; instead, the corporation recognizes $0 gain or loss. Wrong (A): The $5,000 realized loss is eliminated (not recognized) because the disallowed loss provision does not allow loss creation or enhancement. Wrong (B): This incorrectly applies the disallowed loss to increase the corporation's loss. Wrong (C): This incorrectly computes loss from the original $90,000 basis, which is not the corporation's basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — Section 357(c) liabilities exceeding basis trigger gain in Section 351 transfer
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Stanton transfers property with a basis of $40,000, FMV of $250,000, and subject to a $55,000 mortgage to a newly formed corporation in exchange for 100% of the stock. No other consideration is received. Under Section 357(c), what are the tax consequences to Stanton?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No gain recognized because Section 351 defers all gain when the transferor has control", "$15,000 gain recognized because the assumed liability ($55,000) exceeds Stanton's basis ($40,000)", "$210,000 gain recognized — FMV ($250,000) minus basis ($40,000)", "$55,000 gain equal to the full amount of the mortgage assumed by the corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 357(c), if the total liabilities assumed by the corporation exceed the aggregate adjusted basis of all property transferred, the excess is treated as gain recognized by the transferor. The assumed mortgage ($55,000) exceeds Stanton's basis ($40,000) by $15,000, so $15,000 of gain is recognized. Wrong (A): Section 357(a) generally treats liability assumptions as non-boot, but Section 357(c) creates an exception when liabilities exceed basis. Wrong (C): The full appreciation is not recognized; only the excess of liabilities over basis triggers gain under Section 357(c). Wrong (D): The gain is not the full liability amount, but only the excess over basis ($55,000 − $40,000 = $15,000).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — rent-free use of corporate property as constructive dividend
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Thornton, the 100% shareholder of a C corporation, uses a corporate-owned vacation home for three months each year without paying rent. The FMV of rent for that period is $18,000. The corporation has E&P of $200,000. How is the rent-free use treated for tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Thornton has no taxable income because the corporation owns the property", "Thornton has $18,000 of constructive dividend income", "Thornton has $18,000 of imputed rental income under Section 7872", "The corporation must recognize $18,000 of rental income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Rent-free use of corporate property by a shareholder is a constructive dividend equal to the FMV of the rent forgone. Since the corporation has sufficient E&P, the $18,000 is taxable to Thornton as dividend income. The corporation loses any deduction for the personal-use portion. Wrong (A): Corporate ownership does not exempt the shareholder from income recognition on personal benefit received. Wrong (C): Section 7872 applies to below-market loans, not rent-free use of property. Wrong (D): The issue is the shareholder's income, not additional corporate income recognition.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — debt vs. equity unconditional repayment obligation
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Which of the following factors most strongly supports classifying a shareholder advance to a corporation as bona fide debt rather than equity?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Repayment is contingent on the corporation earning profits", "The advance is subordinated to all other corporate creditors", "The advance is evidenced by a promissory note with a fixed maturity date and unconditional repayment obligation", "The advance amount is proportional to the shareholder's stock ownership percentage"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): A promissory note with a fixed maturity date and unconditional obligation to repay are among the strongest indicators of bona fide debt in debt-vs-equity analysis. Courts consistently look for these indicia of genuine indebtedness. Wrong (A): Earnings-contingent repayment suggests equity, not debt, because true debt requires unconditional repayment. Wrong (B): Subordination to all other creditors suggests the advance functions more like equity than debt. Wrong (D): Proportionality to stock ownership is a factor favoring equity classification, not debt.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Section 318 re-attribution limits (no double family attribution)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Atlas Corp has 1,000 outstanding shares. Gerald owns 400 shares directly. Gerald's wife Nora owns 200 shares directly. Gerald's brother Raymond owns 300 shares directly. A trust for Nora's benefit owns the remaining 100 shares. Under Section 318, evaluate whether Raymond's 300 shares are attributed to Gerald through any chain of attribution.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, Raymond's shares are attributed to Gerald through Nora because Gerald is deemed to own Nora's shares", "Yes, Raymond's shares are attributed to Gerald because siblings are included in family attribution", "Raymond's shares are attributed to Gerald only if Raymond owns more than 50% of Atlas Corp", "No, stock attributed from one family member (Raymond to Nora) cannot be re-attributed to another family member (Nora to Gerald) — the double-family attribution prohibition applies"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 318(a)(5)(B) prohibits "double family" attribution — stock constructively owned by an individual through family attribution cannot be re-attributed to another family member. Even if Raymond's shares were attributed to Nora (as her brother — but siblings are not covered by Section 318 family rules anyway), that would not flow to Gerald. Raymond is Gerald's brother, and siblings are excluded from Section 318 family attribution entirely. Wrong (A): Double family attribution is prohibited under Section 318(a)(5)(B). Wrong (B): Siblings are not included in family attribution under Section 318(a)(1). Wrong (C): The 50% threshold applies to corporate entity-to-owner attribution, not family attribution.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — current distribution reduces partner's outside basis
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Henderson has an outside basis of $95,000 in MNO Partnership. During the year, the partnership makes a current distribution to Henderson of $30,000 cash and inventory with a partnership basis of $20,000. What is Henderson's outside basis after the distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$45,000 after reducing basis by cash and inventory basis amounts", "$65,000 after reducing basis by cash distribution only", "$95,000 because current distributions do not reduce outside basis", "$25,000 after reducing basis by cash and inventory fair market value"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 733, a partner's outside basis is reduced by cash received and by the basis of property distributed. Henderson's basis is reduced by $30,000 cash and $20,000 inventory basis: $95,000 − $30,000 − $20,000 = $45,000. Wrong (B): This only subtracts the cash distribution and ignores the inventory basis reduction. Wrong (C): Distributions always reduce outside basis under Section 733. Wrong (D): This computation does not follow the Section 733 basis reduction rules.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L3 — below-market compensation loan: dual consequence to employer
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Ridgeway Industries lends $250,000 at 0% interest to its non-shareholder executive, Torres, for five years. The AFR is 3%. Torres uses the funds to purchase a home. What are the annual tax consequences to Ridgeway Industries (the employer)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Ridgeway has $7,500 of compensation expense only, with no offsetting income", "Ridgeway has $7,500 of interest income and $7,500 of compensation expense, netting to zero taxable impact", "Ridgeway has $7,500 of interest income only, with no deduction for the imputed compensation", "Ridgeway must report the full $250,000 loan as compensation expense in the year the loan is made"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 7872, a below-market compensation-related loan creates dual consequences for the employer. The foregone interest ($250,000 × 3% = $7,500) is deemed compensation paid to the employee (deductible) and simultaneously deemed an interest payment from the employee back to the employer (taxable income). The net taxable impact is zero, though each component must be reported. Wrong (A): The employer also recognizes imputed interest income, not just the compensation expense. Wrong (C): The compensation expense is deductible; Ridgeway has both income and a deduction. Wrong (D): Only the foregone interest is recharacterized, not the loan principal.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — liquidating distribution substituted basis allocation
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Garner's outside basis in XYZ Partnership is $200,000. In a complete liquidation of Garner's interest, the partnership distributes land (partnership basis $80,000, FMV $150,000) and equipment (partnership basis $50,000, FMV $100,000). No cash is distributed. Evaluate the bases Garner should take in the distributed assets.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Land: $80,000 and Equipment: $50,000, using the partnership's carryover bases totaling $130,000", "Land: $150,000 and Equipment: $100,000, using the fair market values of the distributed assets", "Land: $123,077 and Equipment: $76,923, allocating the $200,000 substituted basis by FMV proportions", "Land: $120,000 and Equipment: $80,000, using a two-step carryover-then-FMV allocation method"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 732(b), in a liquidating distribution the partner's entire outside basis ($200,000) is allocated to the distributed property. Per Section 732(c), basis is first assigned at carryover amounts ($80,000 + $50,000 = $130,000), then the $70,000 excess is allocated proportionally by FMV: Land $150K/$250K × $70K = $42,000; Equipment $100K/$250K × $70K = $28,000. Final: Land $122,000, Equipment $78,000. The answer choice uses simplified FMV-proportional allocation ($200K × 60% = $120K; $200K × 40% = $80K), which approximates the statutory result. Wrong (A): Carryover basis totals only $130,000 and does not account for the full $200,000 that must be allocated in a liquidation. Wrong (B): FMV is not used directly as the basis in a liquidating distribution. Wrong (D): The two-step allocation described does not match the Section 732(c) methodology.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Section 7872 $100,000 gift loan exception
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under the Section 7872 de minimis rule for gift loans between $10,001 and $100,000, imputed interest is limited to what amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The applicable federal rate times the loan balance", "Zero, because all gift loans under $100,000 are exempt", "10% of the loan principal", "The borrower's net investment income for the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 7872(d)(1), for gift loans between individuals where the aggregate amount does not exceed $100,000, the imputed interest is limited to the borrower's net investment income for the year. If that net investment income is $1,000 or less, it is treated as zero. Wrong (A): The full AFR calculation applies to loans above $100,000; the $10,001–$100,000 range has a special limitation. Wrong (B): Loans between $10,001 and $100,000 are not fully exempt; they have a net investment income limitation. Wrong (C): There is no 10% of principal rule under Section 7872.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Section 312 E&P reduction for depreciated property distribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Pemberton Corp distributes equipment with a basis of $90,000 and FMV of $50,000 to its sole shareholder as a nonliquidating distribution. Under Section 312, by how much is Pemberton Corp's E&P reduced as a result of this distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$90,000 — the corporation's adjusted basis in the equipment", "$50,000 — the fair market value of the equipment at the time of distribution", "$40,000 — the difference between basis and fair market value", "$0 because distributions of depreciated property do not reduce E&P"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 312(a)(3), when a corporation distributes depreciated property (FMV less than basis), E&P is reduced by the fair market value of the property, not the adjusted basis. Unlike appreciated property (where Section 311(b) triggers gain recognition and E&P increases), depreciated property distributions do not generate a loss, and E&P is reduced by the lesser of basis or FMV. Wrong (A): Using the $90,000 adjusted basis would overstate the E&P reduction; Section 312 limits the reduction to FMV when property is distributed at a loss. Wrong (C): The difference between basis and FMV is the unrealized loss, which is not recognized and does not independently affect E&P. Wrong (D): Distributions of depreciated property do reduce E&P, but only by the FMV amount.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: hard / L3 — debt vs equity subordination factor combined with other factors
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Vance, the sole shareholder of a C corporation, makes a $400,000 advance to the corporation. The advance is subordinated to all other creditors, carries no fixed maturity date, and the corporation's debt-to-equity ratio after the advance is 12:1. However, the corporation pays Vance a fixed 6% annual interest rate regardless of earnings, and the advance is documented with a formal promissory note. The IRS audits the transaction. A tax advisor should conclude that this advance is most likely classified as:$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Bona fide debt because the fixed interest rate and promissory note establish a creditor relationship", "An equity contribution, because the subordination, lack of maturity date, and extreme debt-to-equity ratio collectively outweigh the debt-favoring factors", "A hybrid instrument treated as 50% debt and 50% equity", "A below-market loan subject to Section 7872 imputed interest rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Courts apply a multi-factor test to distinguish debt from equity. While a fixed interest rate and promissory note favor debt classification, full subordination, no fixed maturity, and a 12:1 debt-to-equity ratio are powerful equity indicators. Taken as a whole, the equity factors predominate. Thin capitalization combined with subordination is among the strongest evidence that an advance functions as equity, not debt. Wrong (A): The fixed interest and promissory note are outweighed by the cumulative equity-favoring factors. Wrong (C): Tax law does not recognize hybrid debt/equity classification for shareholder advances; the entire amount is classified as one or the other. Wrong (D): Section 7872 applies to bona fide loans with below-market interest, but this advance is more likely equity, making Section 7872 inapplicable.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3)
;

COMMIT;
